# Migrating `BackgroundProcessor` to .NET 8

## Key Breaking Changes

| .NET Framework | .NET 8 |
|---|---|
| `Thread.Abort()` | **Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** — removed entirely |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Removed |
| `ThreadAbortException` | Never thrown by runtime |
| Manual thread management | Use `BackgroundService` + `CancellationToken` |

## Migrated Code

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;

public class BackgroundProcessor : BackgroundService
{
    private readonly Channel<WorkItem> _queue = Channel.CreateBounded<WorkItem>(100);

    // Called by DI framework automatically
    protected override async Task ExecuteAsync(CancellationToken stoppingToken)
    {
        await foreach (var item in _queue.Reader.ReadAllAsync(stoppingToken))
        {
            try
            {
                await ProcessItemAsync(item, stoppingToken);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (stoppingToken.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Graceful shutdown requested — roll back incomplete work
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break;
            }
            finally
            {
                await FlushBuffersAsync();
            }
        }
    }

    // StopAsync is called by the host; base class cancels the token
    // and waits for ExecuteAsync to complete (with a timeout).
    public override async Task StopAsync(CancellationToken cancellationToken)
    {
        _queue.Writer.TryComplete(); // signal no more items
        await base.StopAsync(cancellationToken);
    }

    public ValueTask EnqueueAsync(WorkItem item, CancellationToken ct = default)
        => _queue.Writer.WriteAsync(item, ct);
}
```

**Registration:**

```csharp
// Program.cs
builder.Services.AddHostedService<BackgroundProcessor>();
```

## Critical Pitfalls

1. **`Thread.Abort()` is gone** — no equivalent exists. You *must* use cooperative cancellation (`CancellationToken`). If `GetNextItem()` blocks without token support, it will hang on shutdown.

2. **Blocking calls need refactoring** — replace blocking `GetNextItem()` with `Channel<T>`, `BlockingCollection` (with token overload), or async I/O.

3. **`volatile bool` is insufficient** — a flag can't interrupt a blocked thread. `CancellationToken` integrates with async waits, `WaitHandle`, and `Channel`.

4. **Graceful shutdown timeout** — the host gives `StopAsync` ~30 seconds by default. Configure via:
   ```csharp
   builder.Services.Configure<HostOptions>(o =>
       o.ShutdownTimeout = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(10));
   ```

5. **Exception handling shift** — catch `OperationCanceledException` instead of `ThreadAbortException`. Unlike `ThreadAbortException`, it does *not* auto-rethrow, so no `ResetAbort()` equivalent is needed.

6. **Don't fire-and-forget in `ExecuteAsync`** — unhandled exceptions will silently stop the service (pre-.NET 8 behavior) or crash the host (.NET 8+ default with `BackgroundServiceExceptionBehavior.StopHost`).

## If You Must Keep Raw Threads

```csharp
private CancellationTokenSource _cts;
private Thread _workerThread;

public void Start()
{
    _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
    _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token)) { IsBackground = true };
    _workerThread.Start();
}

public void Stop()
{
    _cts.Cancel(); // cooperative signal
    if (!_workerThread.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5)))
    {
        // Thread is stuck — log warning; cannot force-kill in .NET 8
        // Consider Environment.FailFast() for truly unresponsive threads
    }
}
```

The fundamental shift: **.NET 8 requires cooperative cancellation** — there is no way to forcibly terminate a thread. Design all blocking operations to accept a `CancellationToken`.
